// linux_x86_64 `Clock` provider closure over the kernel `nanosleep` leaf
// (linux_x86_64 `nanosleep` is 35). This leg does not sleep yet, for two
// reasons. Provider admission classifies an authored syscall leaf's mechanism
// but settles no native realization for it, so target lowering rejects the
// demanded leaf with `MissingBoundarySettlement`. And normalized syscall
// plans place each leaf parameter straight into a syscall register
// (`evaluate_syscall` in `calling-conventions/src/plans/call_plan_evaluation.rs`),
// so the millisecond count would reach `nanosleep` as its `timespec*` request
// pointer. Once authored syscall leaves settle, the darwin leg's shape carries
// over: a checked adapter over a std-private wait requirement that reaches
// `Clock`, realized by the kernel `poll` syscall (7), whose three arguments
// are integers.
linux_x86_64 machine ClockNativeProvider::sleep(milliseconds: u32)
    satisfies Clock::sleep
    via ForeignBinding::Syscall(35);

linux_x86_64 machine ClockNativeProvider::provider_defaults(defaults: &mut ClockNativeProvider) {
    defaults.select_provider<Clock, ClockNativeProvider>();
}
